        <div id="whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Organic Whey Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Truvani Organic Whey is the brand's newer product and the main reason people search for Truvani whey. The vanilla version lists just three ingredients: organic whey protein concentrate, organic vanilla powder and organic monk fruit extract, per retailer listings at the time of writing. Each flavor delivers about 20 g of protein per scoop, according to the brand, and HEB's vanilla label shows about 100 calories and 0.5 g of fat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Truvani says the whey comes from grass-fed, pasture-raised cows on certified USDA Organic farms that are never treated with hormones or antibiotics. Like the plant line, it is free of gums, soy, stevia and added sugar. Vanilla, chocolate and unflavored versions appear on retailer listings, and the unflavored one is a useful base for cooking and for mixing with other powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            From a muscle-building standpoint, the whey is the stronger product. Whey is a complete protein with more leucine per gram than pea, it digests quickly, and dairy proteins generally test lower for heavy metals than plant proteins. If you eat dairy and like Truvani's ingredient philosophy, the whey is the more efficient choice of the two.
          </p>

          <p>
            The caveat is the same 20 g dose. Many grass-fed wheys deliver 24 to 25 g per scoop, so you may want a slightly larger scoop or a cup of milk alongside to reach a 25 g serving. Being a concentrate, it also carries a little more lactose than an isolate, which matters for people with lactose sensitivity; our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains the difference.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Truvani Protein to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you are vegan, dairy-free or simply prefer plant protein, buy the Organic Plant Based Protein in vanilla. It is the flagship, the most versatile flavor for smoothies and oats, and the one with the most buyer feedback. The 20-serving tub is the standard size, and Truvani also sells single-serve packets that are useful for trying a flavor before committing.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you drink shakes on their own, the chocolate plant protein is the more satisfying flavor for many buyers. The trade-off is that chocolate flavors of any plant protein tend to carry more heavy metals than vanilla, because cacao adds its own lead and cadmium on top of what the pea brings. Independent testing of Truvani's chocolate has found these metals, as we cover below, so vanilla is the lower-exposure choice for daily use.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you eat dairy, the Organic Whey is the best Truvani product for most goals. It offers more complete protein per gram, fewer calories per scoop and a shorter label than the plant version. Choose vanilla for shakes or unflavored for cooking. It costs a premium for an organic whey, but organic whey is rare enough that the premium is not surprising.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you pick, check the label on the specific flavor and size you buy. Truvani's formulas are short, but nutrition figures shift a little between flavors, and retailers sometimes show older packaging in their photos.
          </p>
        </div>

