        <div id="metals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Heavy Metals and Prop 65
          </h2>

          <p>
            Truvani's marketing says its ingredients pass the California Proposition 65 heavy metal standard. Independent testing tells a more complicated story. Tamara Rubin, a lead-poisoning prevention advocate who commissions her own lab tests, has published reports saying samples of Truvani's chocolate plant protein and, in June 2026, its vanilla plant protein tested positive for lead, cadmium and arsenic. She also noted that the chocolate product is sold with a Prop 65 warning in California.
          </p>

          <p>
            How worried should you be? Rubin is an advocate rather than a neutral certifier, and her reports focus on whether metals are detected rather than whether they exceed a specific safety limit. Detecting a metal is not the same as finding a harmful dose. Scientists quoted in news coverage of protein powder testing have pointed out that Prop 65 thresholds are far stricter than federal limits, so a warning label means disclosure, not necessarily danger.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bigger context is that plant proteins in general, and chocolate flavors in particular, tend to carry more heavy metals than whey, because peas, seeds and cacao absorb them from soil. Organic certification does not change that, since it governs farming practices, not soil minerals. Truvani is not an outlier here; it is a plant protein behaving like a plant protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            If heavy metals are your main concern, choose the organic whey or vanilla over chocolate, rotate between protein sources rather than drinking one plant powder every day, and ask the brand for recent test results. Pregnant buyers and parents buying for children should talk to a doctor first. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guide covers what certifications and testing actually tell you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Skip Truvani
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip Truvani if you want maximum protein per dollar. At about 20 g per scoop and roughly $2.80 per 25 g for the plant protein, you are paying for organic sourcing and a short label rather than efficiency. Lifters with high protein targets will find better value in a larger-dose organic blend or a conventional whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you compete in a drug-tested sport. We could not confirm NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice certification for Truvani protein at the time of writing. Garden of Life Sport Organic is the organic option to consider instead, because it carries NSF Certified for Sport status per the brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip the plant protein, or limit it, if heavy metals are a priority for you, especially during pregnancy. And skip the whey if you have a milk allergy or are vegan; the plant protein is the dairy-free choice. Anyone with a seed allergy should check the plant label for pumpkin seed and chia.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have kidney disease, take medication or have a medical condition, speak with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding any protein powder. This review is a label analysis, not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

