        <div id="use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Using Truvani Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plant proteins taste and mix best when blended, and Truvani is no exception. A scoop of vanilla blended with frozen banana, a handful of spinach and oat or almond milk makes a smooth shake that hides the earthy notes buyers sometimes mention. Mixed with water in a shaker bottle, expect a thinner, grainier drink.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because the dose is 20 g, plan for it. If you want 25 g or more after training, use a slightly larger scoop, blend it with soy milk, which adds roughly 7 g of protein per cup, or pair the shake with a high-protein food. Plant-protein users generally do well aiming slightly higher per meal than whey users to make up for the lower leucine content.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both the plant protein and the unflavored whey work in oats, pancakes and energy balls. Without gums, the plant powder can thicken as it sits, so mix and drink rather than leaving it in the fridge for hours. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">best protein powder for smoothies</a> guide has more ideas.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, rotate sources if you use protein powder daily. Alternating Truvani's plant protein with its whey, or with whole-food protein such as eggs, yogurt, tofu or beans, is the easiest way to keep any single source's heavy-metal exposure low while still hitting your target. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide helps you set that target.
          </p>
        </div>

